    "q-143434": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143434",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ of four cellphones is drawn from a population. The population has cellphones of two different screen sizes, $4.5\\,\\text{in}$ and $6\\,\\text{in},$ distributed in the ratio $4:1.$ Calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the cellphones in the population have screen sizes of $4.5\\,\\text{in}$ and $6\\,\\text{in},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $4:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $4+1=5$ cellphones only with the following screen sizes:\n\n$$\n4.5\\,\\text{in},\\,4.5\\,\\text{in},\\,4.5\\,\\text{in},\\,4.5\\,\\text{in},\\,6\\,\\text{in}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\cdot 4.5+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(18+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{5} \\\\  & =4.8\\,\\text{in},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\cdot {4.5}^{2}+{6}^{2})-{4.8}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(117)-23.04 \\\\  & =23.4-23.04 \\\\  & =0.36\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}=\\frac{0.36}{4}=0.09\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{0.09}=0.3\\,\\text{in}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
